  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">220998_FY23_WFP_Bangladesh_Volunteer SNSP</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">WVUK have a 50% stake (with WVJ) to this project, working with youth in the Cox&apos;s Bazaar refugee setting. 
 
 The project will target a total of 3,600 youth volunteers of age between 15 to 24 years to participate in different planned interventions &amp;#8211; from trainings, to volunteer services and other youth-centered activities aiming at improving their self-esteem, ensuring a supportive environment for their development and enhancing the overall community resilience. WVB will ensure the approval of need assessment from Camp-in-Charge, RRRC in consultation with WFP.
 
 A total of 3600 youth participants will be provided conditional cash-transfer of BDT 31,000,000 across the project duration, on condition that they participate in the labour activities, life skills training and awareness raising. The transfer modality of the entitlements could be either hybrid (cash &amp; e-voucher) or only one modality (e-voucher/cash). Once the decision is made, the implementation will carry out accordingly.
 
 For this project intervention, WVB will leverage the already established 11 camp-level training under its current WVB - WFP project, &apos;Volunteer Services-Safety Net Systems for the Poorest (SNSP),&apos; thereby ensuring programme efficiency and effectiveness. Wherein systems, capacities, and resources invested in the SNSP programme will also benefit this project. Furthermore, through the proposed project, WVB will secure training centres and youth corners by renovating and maintaining the existing ones. 
 
 Across the hereby proposed project, WVB will lead the implementation of seven key components under the volunteer services in the unregistered camps, as follows: provision of training, provision of awareness sessions, oversight of the light work/labour conducted by the volunteers, provision of cash-based assistance, establishment and management of the youth corners, provision of the mentorship programme and the gender champions initiative.</narrative>
  </description>
